bwfm: the size of the chip's RAM as OpenBSD and Linux read it.

NetBSD masks a bank's size with 0x3f and counts every bank in units of
8 KB. The register has a seven-bit size and a bit (0x200) for banks counted
in units of 1 KB. OpenBSD's bwfm_chip_tcm_ramsize and Linux's
brcmf_chip_tcm_ramsize read it so. A size read wrongly puts the NVRAM,
which goes at the end of the RAM, somewhere other than where the firmware
looks for it. The size found is said on the console.

Applied by tools/netbsd/build.sh to a copy of the file
(docs/tessronos/09-network.md 9.6); NetBSD's tree is not changed.

--- a/sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c
+++ b/sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c
@@ -1661,11 +1661,12 @@
 		    core->co_base + BWFM_ARMCR4_BANKIDX, i);
 		bxinfo = sc->sc_buscore_ops->bc_read(sc,
 		    core->co_base + BWFM_ARMCR4_BANKINFO);
-		ramsize += ((bxinfo & BWFM_ARMCR4_BANKINFO_BSZ_MASK) + 1) *
-		    BWFM_ARMCR4_BANKINFO_BSZ_MULT;
+		ramsize += ((bxinfo & 0x7f) + 1) *
+		    ((bxinfo & 0x200) ? 1024 : 8192);
 	}

 	sc->sc_chip.ch_ramsize = ramsize;
+	printf("bwfm: the chip's RAM is %u bytes, in %u banks\n", ramsize, totb);
 }

 void
